A||||10||loafer87gt||Sickly v6, bad bottom end ||||8||
Z||000000||loafer87gt||01-06-2000||10:51 AM||||I just had my 87 GT in for its regular oil change and the mechanic showed me my old oil and it was full of shavings. I kinda figured something was wrong with my car as it was making a kinda of whining, groaning sound at idle, and did not like to rev past 4000 RPM. The mechanic said it is probably my bearings and rods. Not being the slightest bit mechanically inclined, how big of a deal is this to fix? Do they have to pull my engine to do this work? Will I damage my engine even further by driving it the way it is till spring? Any advice would be appreciated.||||reg||8
Z||000001||fiero56||01-06-2000||11:23 AM||||If the rods and bearings are going out, throwing a rod is very likely. If the bearings and rods are going south, I can't see that engine surviving until spring.||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||01-06-2000||02:05 PM||||Loafer,<P>I hate to be the bearer of bad news... but... I agree with 56.  You will be lucky to get 3000 miles out of that engine.  Essentially this is one of the worst failures that can happen to your cars engine.  Assuming that they are the mains, essentially the engine needs to be removed from the vehicle.  Then the oilpan is removed to gain access to the crankshaft.  Then if there is not much damage (miles on dying engine) one can simply purchase new crank bearings and be done with it.  If the motor is driven, then your bearings will fail allowing your crank to rotate non-concentrically.  That in turn causes your rods/pistons to try to travel in directions that are not aligned with your cylendars.  This situation will lead to several problems such as snapped crankshaft, rod, a rod actually puncturing your block and severe cylendar wall damage.  If you have been entertaining an engine upgrade now is the time to do it.<P>My advice... call Ed Parks at the Fiero Factory in Toney AL.  He will be able to install a 3.4L pushrod in your car for not much more than you could have a 2.8 installed locally.  Of course you have the problem of shipping.  There are others located around the country, NJ, CA, IN and probably more that I am not aware of. <P>Buck up Little Camper,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||8
Z||000003||Wolfhound||01-06-2000||11:18 PM||||If you have noticed no problems, no knocks ,oil pressure OK. Don't take the advice of someone at a Mc Oilchange. If you do have a lack of power do this.<BR>Check your timing chain. If it gets loose it will hit the block around the chain and produce the shavings. I pulled the pan off and found the bearings were fine. Pushed on the timing chain and it was loose as a goose. Replaced it and gears. Car runs fine. Whole new engine to me. It,s not at 100% but that was the main problem. The valve timing was flopping around.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Wolfhound (edited 01-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||01-08-2000||02:24 AM||||A loose timing chain sounds like rocks are floating around in the engine when it's cold and tend to go away after it warms up.<P>You certainly don't want it to break. These are interference engines.<P>If you have more than 150k miles, change it.<BR>I broke two, each around 250k miles. Bent the valve.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||01-08-2000||09:55 AM||||I've never heard of anyone bending the valves when a chain or gear goes on a Fiero. I've always heard the interferance engines are most imports and the Ford Escort. On a few of the other Fiero lists, many people have lost chains or one of the nylon timing gears, with no damage to the valvetrain. Just wondering if you had an isolated instance. Was the valve already been bent, or do the Fiero engines really smack the valves when they loose a chain or gear. Since you mention the chaing going I'm assuming you have the v-6?<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||william_thorniii||01-08-2000||04:30 PM||||Loafer,<P>I admit, perhaps I jumped to conclusions.  The simple fact is that a bad bearing sounds VERY different than a timing chain going.  Either way you had better get it looked at.  <P>56,<P>I didn't think that the 2.8 was an interference engine either... but I haven't checked my manual lately either.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
Z||000007||Wolfhound||01-08-2000||09:03 PM||||What is similar about a bad timing chain and a bad main is both rattle with no engine load. Fooled me! First 2.8 I had.<BR>Coy||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||01-08-2000||10:49 PM||||I've heard that when a chain is <B>really</B> loose, it is evident through the motion of the tach. If you cycle the engine between no load and load, you will see the tach jump as slack is taken out and added. Might be even more evident on a stick?||||reg||1
Z||000009||loafer87gt||01-09-2000||03:47 PM||||Thanks for all your replys guys. I called my Pontiac dealer where the car was purchased new from back in 87 GT and the owner of the dealership is a bit of Fiero fanatic and he is having their shop do an inspection of my car to see whats up. I'll let you guys know what they say after they take a look at her. See ya||||reg||1
Z||000010||william_thorniii||01-12-2000||12:21 AM||||They will probably say that you owe them several hundred dollars!<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
